<header class="lesson">
  <div class="kicker">Lesson 0004 · HFT Trading Practice Repo</div>
  <h1>Kyle's Lambda: Measuring Price Impact</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: this repo just measured, from real ITCH data, that ~10,000 shares of
  net order-flow imbalance moves AAPL about 15 cents. That number — λ — is the price of demanding
  liquidity, and it's the first genuinely microstructural alpha statistic in the codebase.</p>
</header>

<h2>1 · The concept</h2>
<p>In Kyle's (1985) model, a market maker can't tell informed traders from noise traders, so they
protect themselves by moving the price <em>against</em> whichever side is pressing: net buying pushes
quotes up, net selling pushes them down, linearly:</p>
<div class="formula">Δprice = λ · (signed order flow) + noise</div>
<p><strong>λ is the slope</strong>: dollars of price move per share of net flow. It's estimated with
ordinary least squares. High λ = shallow/fragile market (small flow moves price a lot; illiquid,
or lots of informed trading). Low λ = deep market. Three practical decisions turn the idea into an
estimator:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What is "signed flow"?</strong> Here: summed <strong>OFI</strong> (order-flow imbalance,
  Cont–Kukanov–Stoikov best-level definition) — depth added to the bid or consumed from the ask is
  buying pressure (+), and vice versa. Classic alternatives sign the <em>trades</em>; OFI also counts
  quote changes, and empirically explains mid-price moves better.</li>
  <li><strong>What is "Δprice"?</strong> Here: the <strong>microprice</strong> change bar-close to
  bar-close over 1-second bars — each bar is one regression observation (x = bar's OFI sum,
  y = close-to-close microprice change), so consecutive observations telescope the price path
  without double counting.</li>
  <li><strong>One λ or many?</strong> Liquidity varies through the day, so this repo fits one OLS per
  <strong>rolling window</strong> (default 5 min) plus a whole-session aggregate — and any window with
  too few observations is flagged <code>ok=False</code> rather than silently fitted.</li>
</ul>

<h2>2 · Minimal standalone example</h2>
<pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np

rng = np.random.default_rng(<span class="num">0</span>)
TRUE_LAMBDA = <span class="num">2e-6</span>                      <span class="com"># $ per share of net flow (ground truth)</span>

flow  = rng.normal(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">500</span>, <span class="num">2000</span>)       <span class="com"># signed flow per bar (shares)</span>
dpx   = TRUE_LAMBDA * flow + rng.normal(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">2e-4</span>, <span class="num">2000</span>)  <span class="com"># price change + noise</span>

lam, intercept = np.polyfit(flow, dpx, <span class="num">1</span>)   <span class="com"># OLS slope = lambda-hat</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"recovered {lam:.2e} vs true {TRUE_LAMBDA:.2e}"</span>)
<span class="com"># The estimator is 10 lines; the craft is in the OBSERVATIONS —</span>
<span class="com"># what you sign, what you difference, and when you refuse to fit.</span></code></pre>

<h2>3 · In this project</h2>
<p><span class="path">src/oil_alpha/microstructure/kyles_lambda.py</span> — <code>estimate_kyles_lambda()</code>:
events → 1-second bars (<code>groupby(timestamp_ns // 1e9)</code>: x = summed OFI, y = close-to-close
microprice diff) → per-window OLS → <code>LambdaWindow{lam, intercept, r_squared, n_obs, ok}</code> →
session OLS. Sparse bars telescope correctly because OFI is a per-event <em>increment</em>: a missing
second contributes zero flow, and the next bar's close-to-close diff spans the gap.</p>
<p><span class="path">tests/test_kyles_lambda.py</span> — the estimator is validated the same way the
CCF sign convention was: a synthetic stream with an <em>injected</em> λ = 2.5×10⁻⁶ must be recovered
within 5% (signal-to-noise ~25:1, so a sign flip or bar misalignment fails decisively). Plus:
low-observation windows must report <code>ok=False</code>, and an empty frame raises.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Real-day result (AAPL 2019-12-30, regular hours):</strong>
λ = 1.48×10⁻⁵ $/share of OFI, r² = 0.61 over 23,347 one-second bars; all 78 five-minute windows fit,
per-window λ ranging 4.8×10⁻⁶ → 2.6×10⁻⁵ (impact varies ~5× through the day — the rolling-window
decision earning its keep). Magnitudes and r² match the Cont–Kukanov–Stoikov (2014) findings.</div>
<p><span class="path">src/oil_alpha/microstructure/plots.py</span> — <code>lambda_stability_figure()</code>
greys out <code>ok=False</code> windows so an illiquid stretch reads as "no evidence," not "zero impact."</p>

<h2>4 · Often confused: λ (impact) vs the spread (cost)</h2>
<div class="scrollbox"><table>
  <tr><th></th><th>Kyle's λ — price impact</th><th>Bid-ask spread — transaction cost</th></tr>
  <tr><td>What it prices</td><td>Moving the market: cost that <em>grows with size</em></td><td>Immediacy: fixed toll per share, any size that fits at touch</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Who pays</td><td>Anyone trading meaningful size (and everyone after them)</td><td>Every market order, even 1 share</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Scaling</td><td>Linear in flow: 10× size ≈ 10× move (in-model)</td><td>Constant per share</td></tr>
  <tr><td>In this repo</td><td><code>microstructure/kyles_lambda.py</code> (measured: 1.48e-5 $/sh)</td><td><code>config.COST_MODEL</code> half-spread bp (assumed, daily layer)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Also confused:</td><td colspan="2"><strong>OFI vs signed trade volume</strong> — OFI counts quote/depth
  changes too (a canceled ask is buying pressure that never traded); signed volume only counts prints.
  And <strong>r² vs λ</strong>: λ says how hard flow moves price; r² says how much of the price move flow explains.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h2>5 · Practice quiz</h2>
<p>Score: <span id="score">0 / 4</span></p>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q1. Window A: λ = 2.5×10⁻⁵. Window B: λ = 5×10⁻⁶. Where is a 20,000-share buy program more expensive in impact terms?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Lower λ means deeper market — the SAME flow moves price less. B is the cheap window.">Window B — lower λ means each share does more damage</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Impact ≈ λ × flow: A costs ≈ 20,000 × 2.5e-5 = $0.50/share of adverse move vs $0.10 in B. Execute size when λ is low.">Window A — 5× the λ, so roughly 5× the adverse price move</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="λ is exactly the quantity that differentiates them; identical cost would need identical λ.">The same — impact depends on the spread, not λ</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="r² measures explanatory power, not cost level; a window can have high r² and low λ or vice versa.">Cannot say without comparing the windows' r² first</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q2. Why does the estimator regress on bar-close-to-bar-close microprice changes rather than each bar's internal first-to-last change?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Close-to-close diffs telescope the full price path: bar k's y spans exactly the flow in bar k (since OFI increments partition events), so no price movement falls between observations. First-to-last inside a bar drops the move between bars — including the first event's own impact — biasing λ down.">Close-to-close changes partition the price path exactly against each bar's flow; intra-bar changes leak movement between bars</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both need two prices per observation; the computation cost is identical.">It's computationally cheaper to difference closes</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Noise exists either way; the issue is systematic attenuation bias, not noise level.">Intra-bar changes are noisier, close-to-close is smoother</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backwards — close-to-close spans gaps correctly BECAUSE missing bars contribute zero flow; that's why sparse data works.">Close-to-close only works when every second has events</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q3. A 5-minute window has 4 observations and fits λ = 9×10⁻⁴ (60× the session value). What does this repo's estimator report?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Silently returning an absurd fit is exactly the failure mode the design forbids — 4 points can fit anything.">λ = 9×10⁻⁴, since the regression converged</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="n_obs=4 < min_obs=30 ⇒ ok=False: the fitted values are carried for transparency but flagged untrustworthy, and the dashboard greys the bar out.">The window with ok=False — values present but explicitly not to be trusted</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Windows are never dropped — an invisible gap would misread as 'nothing happened' rather than 'not enough evidence'.">Nothing — under-populated windows are removed from the result</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The estimator never substitutes values — that would fabricate agreement with the session estimate.">The session λ, substituted as the window's best estimate</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p>Q4. Session r² = 0.61. What does the other 39% of microprice variance represent?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="0.61 for a one-variable linear model of 1-second price changes is strikingly HIGH — the paper this follows reports similar values as a headline result.">Evidence the linear model failed and λ is meaningless</li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Price also moves on things best-level OFI doesn't see: deeper-book changes, news, cross-asset information, discreteness. λ remains a valid impact slope; r² honestly bounds what flow alone explains.">Price movement driven by information and book changes that best-level OFI doesn't capture</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The estimator's numerics are exact for OLS; r² < 1 is a property of markets, not of float arithmetic.">Numerical error accumulated across 23,347 bars</li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="More windows change granularity, not the session-level share of variance explained by flow.">Variance that would disappear with shorter windows</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<h2>6 · Check your understanding</h2>
<p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Your intraday λ plot shows λ tripling in the last 30 minutes of every
session. Give one liquidity-based explanation and one estimator-based suspicion you'd check first.
<em>(Liquidity: end-of-day inventory unwinding and thinner books make impact genuinely higher.
Estimator: check n_obs and the ok flags for those windows — if closing-auction message patterns cut
